U-Boot loader for the Google Coral Dev Board Mini (MediaTek MT8167S).

The board boots through MediaTek's BootROM, a TF-A BL2 stored in the first
eMMC boot partition, and a FIP (Firmware Image Package) in the "bootloaders"
GPT partition that holds BL31, OP-TEE and U-Boot.  Only the U-Boot part
(u-boot.bin) is built here; BL2, BL31 and OP-TEE come from Google's Mendel
release for the board.

Installing: take fip.bin from a Mendel flashing package, unpack it with
fiptool from Trusted Firmware-A, repack it with this u-boot.bin as the
non-trusted firmware and flash it with fastboot:

  fiptool unpack --soc-fw bl31.bin --tos-fw tee.bin fip.bin
  fiptool create --soc-fw bl31.bin --tos-fw tee.bin \
      --nt-fw /usr/local/share/u-boot/u-boot-coral-dev-board-mini/u-boot.bin \
      fip-freebsd.bin
  fastboot flash bootloaders fip-freebsd.bin

Fastboot mode: ground the KPCOL0 test point while powering the board on.
The stock lk.bin recovery (enable_lk_fastboot.sh) keeps working whatever
is in the eMMC, so a broken U-Boot can always be replaced.

Compared to the Pumpkin MT8516 configuration it is built from, this port
adds the board device tree (8-bit eMMC, 2 GiB of RAM), moves the console
to 115200 baud, keeps the environment in the second eMMC boot partition as
Mendel does, and enables booti, the EFI loader, and RNDIS ethernet over the
USB-C OTG port.  With the USB cable connected to a host running a DHCP and
TFTP server, "run bootcmd_dhcp" fetches and starts loader.efi over the
network; "tftpboot ${kernel_addr_r} kernel.bin; booti ${kernel_addr_r} - -"
starts a FreeBSD kernel.bin directly.

The serial console is on the 40-pin header: pin 6 GND, pin 8 TX, pin 10 RX,
3.3 V, 115200 baud.

For general information about U-Boot see WWW: https://www.denx.de/wiki/U-Boot
